  "cli_result_text": "Submitted the required files, most important first:\n\n**Netty \u2014 channel pipeline exception propagation:**\n1. `transport/src/main/java/io/netty/channel/DefaultChannelPipeline.java` \u2014 core `fireExceptionCaught` propagation and the `TailContext` fallback logging (\"An exceptionCaught() event was fired, and it reached at the tail of the pipeline...\") \u2014 the point where an unhandled exception either gets caught by a handler or is dropped to a WARN log with no further propagation.\n2. `transport/src/main/java/io/netty/channel/AbstractChannelHandlerContext.java` \u2014 `invokeExceptionCaught`/`notifyHandlerException` machinery that walks the pipeline invoking each handler's `exceptionCaught`.\n3. `transport/src/main/java/io/netty/channel/ChannelHandlerAdapter.java` / `ChannelInboundHandlerAdapter.java` \u2014 default `exceptionCaught` behavior (pass-through via `ctx.fireExceptionCaught`), showing how, absent an override, the exception just keeps propagating downstream toward the tail.\n\n**Spring WebFlux \u2014 reactor-netty bridge and error handling:**\n4. `spring-web/.../http/server/reactive/ReactorHttpHandlerAdapter.java` \u2014 the actual bridge between reactor-netty's `HttpServerRequest`/`HttpServerResponse` and Spring's `HttpHandler`; errors from the Netty side surface here as a reactive `Mono` error, logged only at TRACE (`\"Failed to complete: \" + ex.getMessage()`) \u2014 first point where the original Netty exception type/stack gets flattened to a message string.\n5. `spring-web/.../web/server/adapter/HttpWebHandlerAdapter.java` \u2014 `handleUnresolvedError`/`isDisconnectedClientError`: classifies connection-reset/broken-pipe failures via a **substring match on `getMessage()`** rather than exception type, which is exactly where the original Netty exception's identity/context is lost and generically mapped to a 500 or silently dropped.\n6. `spring-web/.../web/server/handler/ExceptionHandlingWebHandler.java` and `WebHttpHandlerBuilder.java` \u2014 the `WebExceptionHandler` chain wiring that runs before `HttpWebHandlerAdapter`'s fallback.\n7. `ReactorServerHttpRequest.java` / `ReactorServerHttpResponse.java` \u2014 request/response wrappers around the reactor-netty types, relevant to how response commitment state affects whether an error can still be turned into a 500.\n8. `ReactorHttpServer.java` (test fixture) \u2014 shows the actual reactor-netty `HttpServer` wiring used with `ReactorHttpHandlerAdapter`.\n\nNote: reactor-netty itself (the intermediate layer between Netty's pipeline and `ReactorHttpHandlerAdapter`) is not one of the two scoped repositories, so the trace necessarily jumps from Netty's `ChannelPipeline` exception propagation directly to where Spring's adapter consumes the resulting reactive error signal.",
